  by: agent  # 26a5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 26a5
  clock: 2  # 26a5
  nonce: 26a5  # 26a5
  message: "CORPUS SIZING from WI-zohuk (2026-10-01). Spelling census (rg, NOT the analyzer) of `.onmessage =` / `.onclose =` in the 36 corpus files (v8 excluded) that construct `new WebSocket(` or `new EventSource(`, cross-checked against an analyzer census (analyze_javascript, HEAD, 42 repos) of edges with call_construct \"assignment\":\n\nEMITTED (receiver bound by `const|let x = new X(...)` in the same function): 4 sites -- argo-cd ui/src/app/shared/services/requests.ts:92 (production), nextjs test/development/app-dir/hmr-iframe/app/page1/subscribeToHMR.ts:39 (test), nestjs gateway.spec.ts:164 and sse-stream.spec.ts:202 (tests; nest, a second clone, repeats both).\n\nMISSED, by the bindings this item names:\n  assignment-bound (`x = new WebSocket(u)` to an outer `let`): beads examples/monitor-webui/web/static/js/app.js:18,30; gitlab app/assets/javascripts/lib/utils/websocket_utils.js:53,57; nestjs/nest gateway.spec.ts:135.\n  assignment-bound WITH a TypeScript annotation on the outer declaration (`let webSocket: WebSocket;` then `webSocket = new WebSocket(...)`): argo-cd ui/src/app/applications/components/pod-terminal-viewer/pod-terminal-viewer.tsx:177,182 (production; `webSocket.send` at :68 lands on external too); lila ui/dgt/src/play.ts:833,842. The declared type alone does not type the receiver: a parameter annotation does (this item's probe), an outer `let` annotation does not (argo-cd survey: `webSocket.send` at :68 and :74 lands on external:send, `webSocket.close()` at :103 on external:close).\n  field-bound (`this.socket.onmessage =` after `this.socket = new WebSocket(...)`): rabbitmq deps/rabbitmq_web_mqtt_examples/priv/mqttws31.js:984,986,1424,1426.\nA SHAPE NOT IN THIS ITEM: a declarator whose initializer is a parenthesized chained assignment, `const ws = (this.ws = new WebSocket(this.lastUrl))` (lila ui/lib/src/socket.ts:153, handlers :156,:171). js_ts.py (~6091) records a constructed type only when the new_expression's PARENT is the variable_declarator; here its parent is the assignment_expression inside a parenthesized_expression, so `ws` is untyped (survey edge check: no edge from socket.ts names the WebSocket module; the field-bound `this.ws.send(message)` at :215 lands on external:send).\nNOT a gap of this item: emscripten src/lib/libwebsocket.js:214,241,405 (`var socket = WS.getSocket(id)`, a factory return); mastodon app/javascript/mastodon/stream.js:251-252 (`new WebSocketClient(...)`), workadventure play/src/front/Connection/RoomConnection.ts:331-332 (`new WorkAdventureWebSocket(...)`) -- project/third-party wrapper classes, not the catalogued WebSocket; phoenix assets/js/phoenix/socket.js:384-387 (`this.conn = new this.transport(...)`, not typable).\n\nTALLY (distinct sites; the nest clone is not double-counted; only the two handler rows WebSocket.onmessage/onclose and EventSource.onmessage): emitted 4; missed through this item's bindings 13 (assignment-bound 5, annotated outer let 4, field-bound 4); missed through the chained declarator 2. On this corpus the receiver binding, not the assignment construct, is what limits the construct's reach. This is a spelling census over files that construct the type; it does not see a receiver constructed in one file and handled in another."  # 26a5
